---
import InnerPage from '../../../components/InnerPage.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Do you handle sidewalk by-law compliance in Toronto?',
    a: 'Yes. Our contractors know Toronto’s regulations and make sure your project complies with local requirements, including sidewalk widths and grades. If the city needs a permit or inspection for your job, we flag it upfront.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can you replace just the damaged sidewalk flags?',
    a: 'Often, yes. If the damage is limited to a few sections, we can remove and repour just those flags and blend the joints. If heaving runs the length of the walk, a full replacement is the better value. We tell you straight which one fits.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What finish do you use on sidewalks?',
    a: 'A broom finish, because it gives reliable grip in wet and icy weather. It is the standard for public-facing concrete and the safest choice for a sidewalk.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do you do sidewalks for businesses?',
    a: 'Yes. We handle commercial concrete jobs including sidewalks, parking spaces and other structures for businesses across Toronto and the GTA.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long until a new sidewalk can be walked on?',
    a: 'Foot traffic is fine after 24 to 48 hours. We barricade the fresh pour and leave clear guidance on curing before we go.',
  },
];
---

